 "postgresql": {
  "overview": "PostgreSQL is an open-source object-relational database server known for standards compliance and extensibility. It is for developers and operators who need a production-grade relational database.",
  "shots": [],
  "when": "- You need an open-source, standards-oriented relational database for a new application.\n- You want a database that runs on Linux with no per-seat licensing questions.\n- You need a server that multiple clients or applications can connect to over the network.\n- You want to learn SQL with a mature, widely documented engine.",
  "how": "- It uses a client/server model: the `postgres` server process manages the data files and accepts connections.\n- Each client connection gets its own server process; clients can run on other hosts over TCP/IP.\n- A server hosts databases, each a separate namespace for tables and other objects.\n- Data lives in tables of rows and typed columns, defined and queried with SQL (`CREATE`, `INSERT`, `SELECT`).\n- `psql` is the interactive client: run SQL and backslash commands such as `\\l` (list databases) and `\\dt` (list tables).",
  "notFor": "- Teams that have already standardized on SQL Server features such as SQL Server Agent or T-SQL-specific code.\n- Embedded, single-file storage inside an app; SQLite is lighter.\n- Very small or throwaway local datasets where a full server adds needless overhead.",
  "commands": [
   {
    "method": "Arch package",
    "command": "sudo pacman -S postgresql",
    "root": "yes",
    "source": ""
   },
   {
    "method": "Create the database cluster (as the postgres user)",
    "command": "sudo -iu postgres initdb --locale $LANG -E UTF8 -D /var/lib/postgres/data",
    "root": "yes",
    "source": ""
   },
   {
    "method": "Start now and at boot",
    "command": "sudo systemctl enable --now postgresql",
    "root": "yes",
    "source": ""
   }
  ],
  "commandsStatus": "Verified against an official source (install section above)."
 },
